  "costUsd": null,
  "content": "Owen's approach play sits below the team and the Tour on strokes gained. By distance, he is ahead of the Division I average from 50 to 125 yards and well behind it from 125 yards out.\n\n**Strokes gained approach.** Over his newest ten rounds, only 8 had strokes gained recorded. He averages -4.16 per round. There is no college figure for strokes gained, so there is no level comparison. The team figure is -3.09, and he ranks 6th of 7 players with a figure. The Tour baseline is 0, which puts him 4.16 strokes behind.\n\n**Proximity by distance.** These figures cover all recorded rounds, not the last ten. The round count behind them wasn't returned, and the team average wasn't available.\n- **50 to 125 yards:** He leaves the ball 23.77 ft from the hole, against the Division I average of 28 ft. He sits at 74 on the level's place measure among 78 college players. The Tour figure is 18 ft.\n- **125 to 175 yards:** He averages 50.58 ft, against the Division I average of 38 ft. His place is 22 among 78 college players. The Tour figure is 30 ft.\n- **175 yards and out:** He averages 94.65 ft, against the Division I average of 55 ft. His place is 13 among 78 college players. The Tour figure is 45 ft.\n\n**Greens in regulation.** Over those same 144 holes he hits 61.11%. That is just above the Division I average of 60% and sits at place 36 among 71 college players. He ranks 5th of 7 on the team, whose average is 62.73%. The Tour figure is 66%.\n\nThe loss comes from mid and long irons, not wedges. His GIR is about at level, but his proximity from 125 yards out is well behind it. Practice time on approach shots from 125 yards and longer is the logical place to start.",
